  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe Erd\u0151s discrepancy problem asks for a step function h(x) on [0,2] with \u222bh(x)dx=1 and h(x)\u2208[0,1] that minimizes:\n   C\u2085 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\nPrevious optimization approaches often get trapped in local optima. Our approach uses a particle swarm optimization (PSO) on a multi-scale grid combined with physics-informed derivatives. Key innovations:\n   - Adaptive resolution switching between low (n=16) and high (n=960) dimensions through gradient flow\n   - Physics-inspired derivative approximations that respect the integral constraint\n   - Lagrangian multipliers to enforce feasible solutions during optimization\n   - Period-2 symmetry exploitation in the correlation function\nBottlenecks addressed:\n   - Optimizes \u22481600 PSO iterations across multiple resolutions\n   - Penalties for solution infeasibility and local stagnation\n   - Limited evaluation count (4 per resolution) to stay within 1300s budget\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   Variables:\n      h(x): step function \u2192 piecewise linear at T=32 points\n      \u03b8: PSO particle position (dim=32)\n   Objective approximation:\n      C\u2085(\u03b8) = max_k \u222b[h(x+k)(1-h(x))]dx\n      \u2192 Mode='full' correlation * dx\n   Constraints:\n      \u222bh(x)dx=1\u2192sum(h_i)*dx=1\n      0\u2264h_i\u22641 (enforced via sigmoid projection)\n   Physics-informed derivatives:\n      \u2202C\u2085/\u2202\u03b8 \u2248 (C\u2085 shift) / (perturbation magnitude)\n   Global minimum search space: n=16-960 samples\n   Solver: PSO(\u03c9=0.9, phip=1.4, phig=1.0) with:\n      position update: x_i(t+1)=x_i(t)+v_i(t)\n      velocity update: v_i(t+1)=\u03c9v_i(t)+phir_i(t)+phiggb_i(t)\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a) Input validation:\n      if not feasible_parents: start with uniform h_i\n      else: use initial_grids(parents)\n   b) Multi-scale PSO loop:\n      1. Low-resolution initialization (n=16):\n          h_base = make_feasible(initial_grids)\n          PSO_particles = population(random(0-1), size=50)\n      2. High-resolution transfer (n=32):\n          h_high = interpolate(h_base)\n      3. PSO iterations (200 steps):\n          For each particle:\n            - Compute C\u2085 via correlation\n            - Project gradients using mode='full'\n            - Apply sigmoid gradients (h_i\u2208[0,1])\n            - Update velocities with: r = random()\n          Track best_global = min(C\u2085)\n      4. Resolution switching (1300s buffer):\n          if best_global improves \u21d2 switch to n=960\n          else \u21cf increase budget/try new seeds\n   c) Restart strategy:\n        Every 10 generations: restart with modified inertia weight (\u03c9=0.7)\n        If stuck (>=10 generations no improvement): random walk mutation\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   - Periodicity enhancement: Alternate trials with sinusoidal perturbations\n   - Dimensionality reduction: Every 50 generations drop n by half (1-layer grid)\n   - Hybrid strategy: Every 300s add 5 complete SLSQP polish steps\n   - Inertia tuning: Start \u03c9=0.9 then reduce by 0.2/cycles beyond 1000s\n   Default cycles: 1000 steps (low-res), 2000 steps (high-res)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - n=16: 100 cycles (4 evaluations)=>~0.1s/iter (400s)\n   - n=32: 500 evaluations/4s (evaluate at full problem)\n   - n=960: 20 evaluations/10s (each C\u2085 costs ~1.3ms initially, ~900ms warmup)\n   Feasibility checks: enforce integral every 5 steps with iterative clipping\n   Budget priority: Allocate 10% to intra PSO, 90% to best_particle updates\n   Fallback: Maintain 5-minute stopping window and cache best_solution\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Objective: C\u2085 \u2248 0.38085\n   - Enforcement: 0\u2264h_i\u22641 with tolerance 1e-5, integrals=0.5*(n_points)*dx\n   - Repair algorithm: Apply make_feasible for violated constraints\n   - Output: Return(h_best, c5_minimum, n_final) (n_final=960 or 32 or 16)\n   - External checks: Recast using mode='full' correlation to ensure\n   - Final verification: e-C5 \u2264 previous_best by tolerance 2e-5",
